00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:26.100
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.100
So I like to start with definitions,

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.416
that way we're on the same page.

00:07:31.416 --> 00:07:35.613
So I'm gonna take the word
trauma, and we're talking about,

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:38.690
in this case, psychological trauma.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:40.540
And much like physical trauma,

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:45.050
something happens to somebody
and there is a reaction.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:49.943
So whereas a physical
trauma, perhaps I trip,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.120
I trip on a nail, I injure myself,

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:55.830
there's a reaction internally.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:08:00.830
In a psychological trauma, an
event happens that I perceive,

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.740
and I perceive it in a way,

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:07.280
when I say, I mean my
nervous system, my body,

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.589
I perceive it in a way that's shocking.

00:08:11.589 --> 00:08:16.589
It is overwhelming, it is threatening.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.050
And as a result, our bodies, our brains,

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:22.620
are gonna try to protect us.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:27.320
So there is a series of
reactions that will happen

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:28.763
in order to deal with that.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:32.770
So what does that mean?

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.283
So for instance,

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:42.190
if I am on my way to work and I witness,

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:46.883
or I myself are involved
in a car accident,

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:50.620
our body knows danger,

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:54.190
and sometimes it can't
necessarily distinguish

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:59.190
between imminent and something
that happened in past.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:04.260
And it will go into
this defensive mechanism

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:05.630
of fight or flight,

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.883
which is very physiological
and it will stay in that.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.540
And so even though the event has passed,

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:17.000
the body hasn't quite
metabolized that event.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.140
So then in a trauma informed education,

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:24.960
what that means that I, the
educator, I have that awareness,

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.320
that trauma is pervasive.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.510
I mean, it impacts us.

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:34.420
It impacts how we show up,
it impacts how much attention

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.990
and energy we can pay
towards, for example,

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:39.470
solving a math problem.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.900
So I, as the educator,

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:46.640
I have that awareness and
I also have the awareness

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:50.800
that there are things I
can do in my classroom

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.800
that can help the student feel
safe, connected, empowered.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:02.930
Those are all really powerful ingredients

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:07.870
to, not necessarily heal
completely from trauma,

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:12.870
but enables us to get a
new start, if you will.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:15.930
- That's a really helpful definition

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:17.610
and there's so many layers to it as well,

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:19.750
I mean, from the
neuroscience side of things,

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.690
to the awareness in the classroom,

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:23.760
to the practices as well.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.350
And then it seems that there
