I'm thinking about the research
that was already going on

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:36.820
in terms of the effects of trauma,

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:39.400
but how that likely was not incorporating

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:41.560
any of that into the classroom.

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:42.860
So, I mean, as a neuroscientist,

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.310
part of me thinks that
could be very frustrating

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.630
to see all this work being done elsewhere,

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:50.910
but not applied in a key
situation where it needs to be.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:51.743
But I wonder, I mean,

00:15:51.743 --> 00:15:53.140
did you feel like you
were building something

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:55.850
from the ground up in terms of awareness

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:57.335
or were there any others who,

00:15:57.335 --> 00:15:58.260
I mean, it didn't sound
like there were many

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:01.430
who were paying attention to
it in this setting at all,

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:03.660
and just wondering what
your experience was like

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:06.160
as you were trying to bring
this to the forefront.

00:16:07.455 --> 00:16:09.550
- I think what was happening,

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:14.550
I was doing it quietly with my students

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:17.270
because of (indistinct)

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:19.866
and because of my experience in emotions,

00:16:19.866 --> 00:16:24.866
and I think generally speaking
in the scientific community,

00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:26.100
it's been my experience

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:29.433
that we don't wanna deal with
emotions, they're sticky.

00:16:30.712 --> 00:16:33.550
And so I was doing it quietly,

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:36.930
doing the research
quietly with my students.

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:41.020
And whenever I would speak
about it at conferences,

00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:45.330
or I would see discomfort

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:51.690
as it's not how we were
trained, a lot of scientists.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:55.710
But I did find

00:16:58.010 --> 00:16:59.970
support from the K through 12.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:03.160
So the K through 12 were
doing trauma informed

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:07.860
before it became something
in higher education.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:10.640
And it was, yes, you are absolutely right,

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:15.640
it was frustrating because I
had the students' experiences,

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:19.750
I had my own experiences,
but also there was a science

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:24.130
that wasn't telling us
that, wait a second,

00:17:24.130 --> 00:17:27.090
learning doesn't work in a vacuum.

00:17:27.090 --> 00:17:30.970
And so frustrating because
I would go to conferences,

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:32.580
read articles, and I think,

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:35.433
why are we not talking about this?

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:42.567
I remember attending a conference in July

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:48.620
of 2017 and the conference had
many tracks, active learning,

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:57.610
metacognition, this
and that and the other.

00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:01.050
And there was not a single,
and it was about STEM.

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:05.140
And it was not a single,
not a single session

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:09.063
about what's going on with DACA students?

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:12.810
Now for me, I was working
(indistinct) many of them,

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:17.810
and I could see that it was
the socio-political situation

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:21.053
was impacting how they show up.

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:24.360
And so for me to just go and say,

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:26.620
active learning, metacognition,

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:28.020
this and that and the other,

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.940
it doesn't take the whole
context into account.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:35.333
There was serious trauma going on.

00:18:37.221 --> 00:18:39.430
So yes, frustrating.

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:41.640
But when you're working with students

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:45.380
and you see how they respond
and how they give back,

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:47.113
it's worth it.

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:50.123
- Definitely in a lot of ways.

00:18:50.123 --> 00:18:52.570
And I'm sure that you've
gotten that feedback

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:53.760
from students over the years.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:55.890
And then just seeing,

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:57.470
I mean, I'm thinking also,
just as you described,

00:18:57.470 --> 00:18:59.090
the empowerment from that as well,

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:01.270
just even that shift about
what you talked about earlier

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:04.160
with the (indistinct)
not what's wrong with me,

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:06.210
but being able to actually think back

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.710
about what happened to me
and how is that impacting

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:10.210
how I'm moving forward,

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:15.210
I mean, there's also a sense
of removal of blame in a sense,

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:17.150
and just having the
(indistinct) to move forward.

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:19.660
So, I mean, on that note,
what are some of the feedback

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.270
that you've gotten from
students and learners

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:24.540
as you've been engaged in this work

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:26.390
and really bring it to the forefront?

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:32.390
- Yeah. So one that has
been I think powerful for me

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:35.293
and also hearing from students.

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:40.220
So Dr. Addy, you're a neuroscientist

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:44.520
and you do this work.

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:48.600
We know that there are mechanisms
that are turned on or off

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:51.660
when someone undergoes trauma.

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:54.930
Now, there's research that shows that

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:59.930
it's actually the vestibular
system is impacted.

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:02.393
And the vestibular system,

00:20:04.655 --> 00:20:08.210
it can impact our sense of being in space,

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:12.430
our sense of agency (indistinct)

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:16.400
So I've been fascinated
with that literature.

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:19.810
And also thinking about
when someone experienced

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:24.810
this trauma, our sense of
control or helplessness

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:26.983
feels shattered.

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:33.330
It's really important
to cultivate the space

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:37.460
or an environment where someone
feels they have choices,

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:39.773
option, agencies.

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:44.860
This has been really in terms
of feedback from students,

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:46.920
this has been one feedback,

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:50.430
I would say even more than
when they talk about safety,

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:55.430
the sense of I have a say, I have agency,

00:20:56.480 --> 00:20:58.740
seems to be really powerful

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:03.740
in terms of helping students
feel that there is a way out,

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:05.367
there is a way (indistinct).

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:07.967
- And it's so important.

00:21:07.967 --> 00:21:12.510
And it's helpful to hear what
has been kind of a salient

00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:15.230
piece for the students from
their perspective as well.

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:17.380
On that note, if we can shift a little bit

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:19.010
to give people some of that practical,

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:23.040
I'm curious if you can share
how you encourage people

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:26.660
to bring that awareness
into the classroom as well.

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:27.493
Because, I mean,

00:21:27.493 --> 00:21:29.740
I think that's underlying a lot
of what you've talked about,

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:31.180
that the awareness just hadn't been there,

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:32.920
maybe it had been in K through 12,

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:34.340
it had been in other pockets,

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:35.670
but not in a classroom setting

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:37.160
where there's really the stigma

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:38.740
of something that's wrong with you,

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:41.410
and it's more about deficits that you have

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:42.830
as opposed to what happens to you,

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:45.580
how do you shift or how
have you been shifting that?

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:50.830
- So I've been encouraging
people to read, learn,

00:21:50.830 --> 00:21:55.713
listen about trauma, including
intergenerational trauma.

00:21:56.930 --> 00:22:01.750
And my hope there is to
you become the subject,

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:04.883
you read or hear something, and you think,

00:22:05.837 --> 00:22:07.307
"I experienced that."

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:13.220
And this I experienced
that can then help bring,

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:17.430
not just awareness about me,

00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:20.773
but about who else is experiencing that.

00:22:22.168 --> 00:22:25.430
And, so I tell my colleagues,

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:28.480
I tell family members and friends,

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:30.670
if you're doing the
laundry in the background,

00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:35.340
just have something playing so
you could learn about trauma,

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:37.393
learning about the body. Why does it?

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:41.070
And the reason for that is not just,

00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:43.340
I have awareness about
what happens in the body,

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:46.530
it's not an academic endeavor,

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:50.500
but it's really to help us then recognize

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:53.113
and have the self-awareness
when we're triggered,

00:22:53.990 --> 00:22:57.180
what's going on, what
can we learn from that?

00:22:58.280 --> 00:23:01.580
And then I say, what do
you think is happening

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:02.480
with the students?

00:23:03.786 --> 00:23:06.760
There are things in the background

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:09.410
just like there are things
in the background for you.

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:11.940
So I start with that.

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:13.977
I have been really intentional to say,

00:23:13.977 --> 00:23:17.180
"Learn about this, it's empowering."

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:20.730
We tell students, knowledge is power,

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.070
knowledge is empowering,

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:24.360
and knowledge can also be healing

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:29.283
because you listen and you
think for me, certainly like,

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:33.010
wait, there's nothing wrong with my brain,

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:37.053
it was just stuck because of the event.

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:43.560
And then I also encourage colleagues to,

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:51.890
on the one hand, I say, "How
would you like to be treated

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:54.510
if you were the student in that class?"

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:58.370
And more so I say, "Talk
with your students,

00:23:58.370 --> 00:24:01.227
how do they want to be treated?"

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:04.300
A lot of times we make assumptions

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:07.070
that the students (indistinct)
that we care about them,

00:24:07.070 --> 00:24:08.263
we have their back.

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:10.833
But when things get tough,

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:17.070
the brain will go into
this constricted narrow

00:24:17.070 --> 00:24:20.420
and it may not be able to remember.

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:23.817
So let the students know that,

00:24:23.817 --> 00:24:27.150
"I'm so excited you're in this class,

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:29.953
we're on a journey
together. I have your back.

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:35.020
I am invested in your success."

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:38.370
Repeat that so it can become part

00:24:38.370 --> 00:24:40.973
of the DNA makeup of the class.

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:47.040
And then learn about the students,

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:50.790
learn about the human beings who are...

00:24:51.850 --> 00:24:54.463
And learn in a variety of ways.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:25:00.070
Perhaps a survey, perhaps email,

00:25:00.070 --> 00:25:03.083
perhaps asking them to
come and meet with you,

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:10.110
learning about them will then enable,

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:12.590
I could give you a list
of do's and don'ts,

00:25:12.590 --> 00:25:14.953
and I have suggestion to offer.

00:25:16.040 --> 00:25:19.030
And at the same time, it can't
be a one size that fits all,

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:21.213
'cause context (indistinct) so important.

00:25:23.679 --> 00:25:25.450
- Yeah. I think that's really well said.

00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:26.420
And it makes me think about,

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:28.270
even as you were going
through your introduction,

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:30.890
just the way that you
are seeking to educate

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:32.600
the whole student.

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:33.833
So I love the framework of really

00:25:33.833 --> 00:25:35.127
learning about them as well,

00:25:35.127 --> 00:25:36.770
'cause it seems like also sometimes

00:25:36.770 --> 00:25:38.070
there's a disconnect there

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:41.880
between maybe what an instructor's
deal that they're doing

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:45.110
or want to do versus
what students perceive.

00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:48.380
'Cause I can imagine situations
where instructors may say,

00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:49.213
just say things like,

00:25:49.213 --> 00:25:51.560
"Oh, we care about how you're doing,

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:53.240
and we care about your success."

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:55.850
But it's all within the framework

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:58.000
of the academic achievement,

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.010
as opposed to caring
about you as a person.

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:03.388
And just to share, I mean,
this is all anecdotal,

00:26:03.388 --> 00:26:05.860
not evidence-based per se,

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:09.300
but just the number of
students who share with me

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:12.460
in situations where we've
talked about their feelings

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:14.370
with everything that's happened
over the last few years

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:16.210
and students that have never had

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:20.340
any professor actually create
that space to hear how I feel

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:22.340
about the situation or
give a space to talk about.

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:24.280
So it makes me think about your comments

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:26.250
about asking how they're doing.

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:28.400
Whereas as an instructor,

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.210
an instructor may feel, "Well,
I gave those opportunities

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:32.340
by saying that I was invested in them."

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:34.670
But it's a disconnect between saying it

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:39.100
and actually taking a pause to
invest and hear the feedback.

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:41.680
So again, I'm sure there's a
lot you could elaborate on that

00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:43.130
from the work that you've done,

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:44.560
but this is something I've noticed

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:46.750
just as engaging with undergraduates

00:26:46.750 --> 00:26:49.120
and graduate students over the years.

00:26:49.120 --> 00:26:50.830
- Yeah. And it is foreign.

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:53.603
I remember one time I asked my student,

00:26:54.547 --> 00:26:56.160
"How's your heart?"

00:26:56.160 --> 00:27:00.567
And he was, "What?
Physiologically, what do you mean?"

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:06.730
And I had to explain to
them what I mean by that

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:12.910
and why it's so important
to do that checking.

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:18.410
And I started like having a
variety of ways of approaching

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:24.240
and sometimes asking the question,
how are you? Is so tough.

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:29.300
I know that there are days
when you ask me that question,

00:27:29.300 --> 00:27:32.570
it's overwhelming, I don't
know how to (indistinct).

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:37.570
And so I started for example
saying how bad is this Monday?

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:45.150
Or using emojis and actually,
a student recommended emojis.

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:52.240
But the point is, it's
modeling that you care enough

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:55.250
to allocate the time,

00:27:55.250 --> 00:27:58.653
just like you did at the
beginning of this conversation,

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:01.530
asking where I am, how I am

00:28:01.530 --> 00:28:06.530
and modeling and then
pausing to listen. Yeah.

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:12.783
- Yeah. I think it's so important.

00:28:12.783 --> 00:28:15.395
I also appreciate the
sensitivity that you mentioned

00:28:15.395 --> 00:28:16.670
in terms of the how are you

00:28:16.670 --> 00:28:19.420
and how difficult that can be at times.

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:22.357
I've noticed sometimes people
also will give space to say,

00:28:22.357 --> 00:28:24.160
"Well, it's fine if you
don't want to answer,

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:26.950
but I want to at least make
that option available."

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:29.500
'Cause even thinking back
to the last few years

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:32.310
with several of my students
of color in particular,

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:34.650
I mean, long before the
unfortunate situation

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:35.483
with George Floyd,

00:28:35.483 --> 00:28:38.670
but just them often feeling
they didn't have the space

00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:42.040
for anyone to actually check
in and see how they were

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:43.630
when these (indistinct)
things were happening.

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:45.212
So even if they didn't want to
share it, just to know that,

00:28:45.212 --> 00:28:48.760
okay, someone actually
noticed and took the time,

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:50.540
then maybe I can come back to that later.

00:28:50.540 --> 00:28:54.030
At least they felt seen
in that moment as well.

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:54.863
And so again, I mean,

00:28:54.863 --> 00:28:56.220
it just seems like so many of the things

00:28:56.220 --> 00:28:57.940
that you're trying to make sure

00:28:57.940 --> 00:28:59.560
that those really come to the forefront

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:01.850
and there is that space as well,

00:29:01.850 --> 00:29:03.750
which I think is critically important.

00:29:05.380 --> 00:29:06.213
- Definitely.

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:10.980
- So I know you mentioned
not necessarily giving

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:12.730
all the practicals,
it's not cookie cutter,

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:15.740
even as you mentioned from
your tough love situation,

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:18.260
but other general practical components

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:21.160
that you do like to mention

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:22.600
to people who are in classroom settings

00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:23.950
about what are some of the practices

00:29:23.950 --> 00:29:27.710
once you have that awareness
to really empower the students

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:31.203
to make sure that it's at
the forefront and integrated?

00:29:32.055 --> 00:29:37.055
- So let's start with options
and choices, voice and choice.

00:29:37.340 --> 00:29:40.750
It's really important
for reminding student

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:45.210
that they agency, that
they have a sense of self.

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:47.370
So how would this work?

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:50.170
So I teach a physiology class.

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:54.460
And in a physiology class, I
assigned a research project,

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:56.253
and I pick the topic.

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:01.370
And it occurred to me a few years ago,

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:03.143
why am I limiting the topic?

00:30:05.560 --> 00:30:08.690
If it's about what they are excited about

00:30:08.690 --> 00:30:11.210
and what they feel connected,

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:16.107
why don't I offer a series
of suggestions and then say,

00:30:16.107 --> 00:30:19.333
"And if you want to come up
with your own research question,

00:30:19.333 --> 00:30:21.660
work with me, we will."

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:25.980
And what I saw when that
happened, when I did that,

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:30.430
is there was more
investment in the project

00:30:30.430 --> 00:30:32.180
because now it's like,

00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:36.190
now I have this kidney pain
that is like comes and goes,

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:39.580
I wanna research that,
versus the cookie cutter

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:44.523
one size fits all question.

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:48.150
So that's one.

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:53.150
The other thing is I have
flexibility is so important.

00:30:55.610 --> 00:31:00.610
And so flexibility would
be we're in a pandemic.

00:31:01.380 --> 00:31:06.380
I mean, I am so proud of the
students for just showing up.

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:11.990
It's overwhelming, we feel
overwhelmed, I feel overwhelmed.

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:15.153
Sometimes it's hard to get out
of bed and yet they show up.

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:21.320
And so we want to have
positive reinforcement.

00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:24.290
So every class, I make it a point

00:31:24.290 --> 00:31:27.507
to give positive reinforcement
to the whole class.

00:31:27.507 --> 00:31:29.600
"You're here, I'm so excited."

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:34.440
Today I had a class and all
the students were there.

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:37.143
I thought I was in seven heaven.

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:42.430
So that positive reinforcement matters.

00:31:42.430 --> 00:31:44.173
And at the same time,

00:31:45.297 --> 00:31:49.083
let the students know that life happens.

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:53.340
And when life happens and you fell asleep

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:58.750
and you couldn't do the paper on time

00:31:58.750 --> 00:32:00.720
because you were also, I don't know,

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:05.570
helping your father or
something, come see me,

00:32:05.570 --> 00:32:07.223
we will work something out.

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:11.490
There is a tendency
among educators to say,

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:13.770
all students are gonna
take advantage of this.

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:18.250
And they're going to, I've
never, I've been teaching for,

00:32:18.250 --> 00:32:20.510
I don't know, over 10 years,

00:32:20.510 --> 00:32:23.460
I've never had a student
take advantage of it,

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:26.230
or even give me the sense that
they were taking advantage.

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:28.557
On the contrary, when I say,

00:32:28.557 --> 00:32:31.120
"Well, do you wanna take an extension?"

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:32.877
They have like a little bit of anxiety.

00:32:32.877 --> 00:32:34.737
"No, no, no, I'm gonna fall behind."

00:32:36.340 --> 00:32:41.340
So talking about when
life happens, flexibility,

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:43.560
it doesn't mean the students
are gonna take advantage

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:44.580
or take you up on it,

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:46.933
but they know that there is that comfort.

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:55.400
So in addition to that,
I am really before COVID,

00:32:57.240 --> 00:33:01.823
I'm really intentional to center

00:33:03.220 --> 00:33:06.070
social and emotional and mental health.

00:33:06.070 --> 00:33:09.240
So one exercise or one practice I do

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:13.210
is that during the first or the
second week of the semester,

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:16.680
I just talk very little about
social and emotional learning

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:20.173
or social (indistinct)
learning is holistic and so on.

00:33:21.035 --> 00:33:24.190
And I also invite someone
from the counseling center,

00:33:24.190 --> 00:33:26.703
either the counseling
center or a therapist,

00:33:27.640 --> 00:33:30.390
and I have a conversation with the person

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:32.993
and I say things like,

00:33:35.530 --> 00:33:39.240
sometimes I get really
triggered, what's going on?

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:41.940
Or sometimes I'm sweating
and I have anxiety,

00:33:41.940 --> 00:33:43.270
what's going on.

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:48.190
And the person then, well
funny that you asked,

00:33:48.190 --> 00:33:50.810
I'm so glad, and the person then gives

00:33:50.810 --> 00:33:55.810
a layman explanation of mental health

00:33:56.090 --> 00:33:59.630
and why physical health
we need to attend to it.

00:33:59.630 --> 00:34:02.450
And then I have the student program

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:05.350
in their phone numbers.

00:34:05.350 --> 00:34:07.790
Who do you text when you have anxiety

00:34:07.790 --> 00:34:10.610
in the middle of the night?
There are free resources.

00:34:10.610 --> 00:34:13.120
I wanna demystify it.

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:15.870
I want to demystify mental health.

00:34:15.870 --> 00:34:18.073
I want to also (indistinct) it.

00:34:21.870 --> 00:34:26.870
I want to show these students
that this is so important.

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:30.831
We're gonna have a session about this.

00:34:30.831 --> 00:34:34.720
This is also to preemptively have a plan,

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:37.573
when things get really
tough, they have a plan.

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:43.930
Now, one thing I wanna say
about all of these things

00:34:43.930 --> 00:34:48.930
is there's a lot that the
faculty member is doing.

00:34:50.640 --> 00:34:52.480
They're doing their lesson plans,

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:56.230
they're doing their lectures,
they're doing their grading.

00:34:56.230 --> 00:34:59.633
And also they're doing
these emotional giving.

00:35:00.870 --> 00:35:03.040
And so it's a lot of work.

00:35:03.040 --> 00:35:07.860
And what I have experienced
is that it gives back,

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:09.230
what do I mean by that?

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:14.180
When you look and you see
your students feel safe,

00:35:14.180 --> 00:35:17.193
connected, they're
laughing, they're engaged,

00:35:18.867 --> 00:35:21.267
you look at it and you
say, it was all worth it.

00:35:22.961 --> 00:35:27.961
I am part of a tiny ripple effect

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:34.083
that is going to hopefully make an impact.

00:35:36.613 --> 00:35:38.970
- I love that ripple
effect comment as well.

00:35:38.970 --> 00:35:40.990
'Cause it ties everything together,

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:44.170
which you mentioned and
just the back and forth

00:35:44.170 --> 00:35:45.240
that can come from that as well.

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:47.430
It makes me think also
what you talked about

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:51.580
how those things can have a
sense of healing for you as well

00:35:51.580 --> 00:35:54.040
in your own process and
for us as instructors,

00:35:54.040 --> 00:35:55.340
and it's really important.

00:35:57.550 --> 00:36:00.230
- Yeah. I grew up in a tradition.

00:36:00.230 --> 00:36:03.550
My grandma used to use a lot of poetry

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:09.003
and I grew up in her saying
that we're each other's mirror.

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:15.483
And the state of the students' wellness

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:18.353
and the faculty wellness,

00:36:19.829 --> 00:36:23.960
they're very similar,

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:26.400
there's pain and anxiety.

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:31.400
And even in some cases,
romanticizing despair.

00:36:34.387 --> 00:36:38.717
So I could look at the student
and see my own reflection.

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:45.250
And yet I could use the
power of the mirror neurons

00:36:45.250 --> 00:36:49.723
to shift that (indistinct) back to me.

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:52.643
- Definitely powerful.

00:36:52.643 --> 00:36:55.290
And I love that you tied the
neuroscience back in too.

00:36:56.228 --> 00:36:58.230
(laughs)

00:36:58.230 --> 00:36:59.320
One question was I've had,

00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:01.400
and I don't know how much
of this is in the field,

00:37:01.400 --> 00:37:04.410
which you started to touch
on all the importance

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:08.310
of the level of the student,
but just talking to,

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:09.310
thinking about my own kids,

00:37:09.310 --> 00:37:11.210
talking to colleagues and their kids,

00:37:11.210 --> 00:37:13.010
and thinking about all
the way through college,

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:16.120
just outside thinking
about all the stressors

00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:19.270
that the teachers and
instructors are going through.

00:37:19.270 --> 00:37:22.140
And sometimes again, this is
all speculation anecdotal,

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:25.420
but thinking that even those individuals

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:29.120
seem to be operating where the
stress is also affecting them

00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:31.390
and how they interact in the classroom

00:37:31.390 --> 00:37:34.090
and sometimes negatively
without maybe not necessarily

00:37:35.010 --> 00:37:35.910
people being aware of it.

00:37:35.910 --> 00:37:38.480
So I wonder even as you were
talking about mirroring,

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:43.050
are there ways that some of
that awareness can also happen

00:37:43.050 --> 00:37:46.140
for instructors and for
teachers to be able to have

00:37:46.140 --> 00:37:47.270
some of that self-awareness,

00:37:47.270 --> 00:37:51.300
think about how they
approach their students

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:53.460
in the classroom, even as
they're trying to be attentive

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:54.293
to the students as well.

00:37:54.293 --> 00:37:56.560
And obviously that's a lot to think about

00:37:56.560 --> 00:38:00.030
in terms of cognitive load
and emotional bandwidth,

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:03.543
but I'm curious if that
comes into play in your work.

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:08.620
- Secondary traumatic stress is real.

00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:11.530
We have a lot of the literature

00:38:13.070 --> 00:38:16.490
comes from healthcare workers

00:38:16.490 --> 00:38:21.490
and volunteers that work
with refugees and immigrants.

00:38:23.650 --> 00:38:28.650
And again, because we're so
empathic, we are like a sponge.

00:38:28.920 --> 00:38:31.660
And so we pick (indistinct) people's some

00:38:33.150 --> 00:38:38.150
stress and distress and also
the helplessness feeling that,

00:38:39.217 --> 00:38:41.380
"Oh, I wanna help the
student, but I can't."

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:44.840
And then it just, it
wears heavy on our hearts.

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:49.840
And in addition, we might be
having our own independence.

00:38:50.870 --> 00:38:55.790
So they accumulate, and
then it impacts as you said,

00:38:55.790 --> 00:38:57.320
how we show up.

00:38:57.320 --> 00:38:59.687
And so I tell my colleagues, I say,

00:38:59.687 --> 00:39:01.760
"Do this experiment,"
actually don't do it.

00:39:01.760 --> 00:39:03.320
I mean, I don't advocate it,

00:39:03.320 --> 00:39:06.510
but go to the class sleep deprived

00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:10.530
and see how much patients you have

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:14.730
and then ask the students,
"Was I different?"

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:19.300
Of course, in fact, my students know

00:39:19.300 --> 00:39:21.560
when I'm having a difficult day,

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:26.093
no matter how much I work to mask it.

00:39:26.950 --> 00:39:31.140
So what that means is that
this whole notion of self care

00:39:31.140 --> 00:39:35.360
is not just (indistinct) it's a necessity.

00:39:35.360 --> 00:39:40.360
If I am going to want to
continue to give and support

00:39:40.610 --> 00:39:45.610
and show up holistically for
my students, I have to pause.

00:39:48.130 --> 00:39:51.810
I have to pause and
take care and replenish,

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:53.717
however that is for you.

00:39:55.890 --> 00:39:57.153
- Yeah. So important.

00:39:58.060 --> 00:40:02.150
And I imagine that sleep
deprivation experiment has happened

00:40:02.150 --> 00:40:03.668
on several occasions.

00:40:03.668 --> 00:40:05.751
(laughs)

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:13.110
- If I'm sleep deprived
and I show up to class

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:17.713
and actually, it happens,

00:40:18.559 --> 00:40:22.320
it was so perfect the way it
happened that I actually pause

00:40:22.320 --> 00:40:25.433
and I let the students
see what was happening.

00:40:27.700 --> 00:40:31.060
They can ask me the same
question 3, 4, 7 times.

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:33.330
And I just repeat it and I make a,

00:40:35.234 --> 00:40:37.623
hearted comments of it.

00:40:38.777 --> 00:40:41.230
It was a day where I was sleep deprived

00:40:41.230 --> 00:40:43.160
and they asked the same question,

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:45.090
and by the third time they asked it,

00:40:45.090 --> 00:40:48.800
I had this, "What, are you kidding me?"

00:40:48.800 --> 00:40:50.480
And I felt it.

00:40:50.480 --> 00:40:54.203
And then I let the students
look at what was going on.

00:40:56.290 --> 00:41:01.290
So it is important that we pay attention

00:41:02.220 --> 00:41:06.680
to how the educators
are and to empower them

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:10.590
to take care of their own
wellbeing, to replenish.

00:41:10.590 --> 00:41:15.590
And also parents, when I
speak with my family members,

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:18.360
I say, "What are you doing
to take care of you?"

00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:23.360
So they could continue to
help their kids and yeah.

00:41:23.620 --> 00:41:26.830
- It's so important and
encouraging to hear how you

00:41:26.830 --> 00:41:29.250
incorporate it on both sides,

00:41:29.250 --> 00:41:31.390
which again makes me just think about even

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:34.170
all the different things
that you're doing.

00:41:34.170 --> 00:41:37.270
And just, I mean, in terms
of the research, the teaching

00:41:37.270 --> 00:41:39.690
and helping your colleagues,
informing your colleagues,

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:42.740
helping your students,
And so I can imagine,

00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:45.370
I mean, were there some
of your own practices,

00:41:45.370 --> 00:41:47.600
if you're willing to share
just with everything,

00:41:47.600 --> 00:41:48.470
because as you mentioned,

00:41:48.470 --> 00:41:50.670
there's a lot of empathy
that comes with that too.

00:41:50.670 --> 00:41:52.284
And it seems that it would be very easy

00:41:52.284 --> 00:41:54.480
for you to be carrying out a lot.

00:41:54.480 --> 00:41:56.220
I know I can say that from
my own experience too,

00:41:56.220 --> 00:41:59.830
in a different space, but
as talking with our BiPAP

00:41:59.830 --> 00:42:03.990
students and faculty, sometimes
all that weight can sit,

00:42:03.990 --> 00:42:08.170
if there's not ways to at
least process and manage that

00:42:08.170 --> 00:42:10.600
before the next event or
the next mentoring session

00:42:10.600 --> 00:42:12.470
or the next teaching session.

00:42:12.470 --> 00:42:13.370
So I'm just curious for you

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:17.577
how do you go about that
on a day-to-day basis?

00:42:18.959 --> 00:42:23.150
- So I can't regulate what I don't know.

00:42:23.150 --> 00:42:27.200
And so this self-awareness
is so important tuning in,

00:42:27.200 --> 00:42:31.480
and I started paying attention to

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:36.480
I know where the pain shows
up and when I have not,

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:40.863
or when I feel I'm just really tense,

00:42:42.750 --> 00:42:46.250
it's an indication 'cause I documented it.

00:42:46.250 --> 00:42:50.630
I wanted to, if there is an
indication that I am just

00:42:50.630 --> 00:42:54.210
going, going, going, no pausing,
no pausing, no processing,

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:55.363
no replenishing.

00:42:57.200 --> 00:43:01.620
And then I just, I mean,
it's really interesting.

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:04.193
I think, let me just go one more day.

00:43:05.170 --> 00:43:07.620
I'll take time this weekend
or the next weekend.

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:11.827
And it reaches a point
where for me, my body says,

00:43:11.827 --> 00:43:14.277
"You're not gonna stop,
I'm gonna make you stop."

00:43:15.750 --> 00:43:19.330
And it shows up in the form
of really migraine headaches

00:43:19.330 --> 00:43:21.830
or tension headache or this or that,

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:23.880
where I'm no longer effective.

00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:25.687
It doesn't matter how much (indistinct).

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:32.490
So what I started doing, I have
to hold myself accountable.

00:43:33.920 --> 00:43:36.920
And so I have a group of friends,

00:43:36.920 --> 00:43:40.190
family members that they check up with me,

00:43:40.190 --> 00:43:44.853
we check up on each other and
we feel comfortable saying,

00:43:46.707 --> 00:43:48.610
"I haven't been doing too much,

00:43:48.610 --> 00:43:51.140
or what are you doing to
take care of yourself?"

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:56.140
And they can tell when
I'm not answering fully.

00:44:00.130 --> 00:44:04.427
I also learned what helps me replenish,

00:44:06.170 --> 00:44:09.530
and for me it's disconnecting

00:44:09.530 --> 00:44:12.173
and it could be for half an hour only.

00:44:14.050 --> 00:44:18.510
I remember a mentor saying
to me, "Take five minutes."

00:44:18.510 --> 00:44:20.160
And initially I thought five minutes,

00:44:20.160 --> 00:44:21.450
what is five minutes gonna do?

00:44:21.450 --> 00:44:24.300
Five minutes, if it becomes a routine,

00:44:24.300 --> 00:44:25.540
and it's just for you,

00:44:25.540 --> 00:44:28.830
and you're just focusing on, I don't know,

00:44:28.830 --> 00:44:33.160
breathing or dancing or
something, is powerful.

00:44:33.160 --> 00:44:38.160
And so for me, every day in my calendar,

00:44:39.530 --> 00:44:44.530
I allocate half hour and that half hour

00:44:45.340 --> 00:44:48.670
I don't use the phone or the computer,

00:44:48.670 --> 00:44:52.980
or I just, I do something with nature.

00:44:52.980 --> 00:44:57.900
Even if it, I was in
Detroit visiting my family

00:44:57.900 --> 00:44:59.330
and it was snowing

00:45:01.043 --> 00:45:06.043
and I would practically
put every layer I have on

00:45:06.270 --> 00:45:08.863
so I could go outside
just for half an hour.

00:45:10.550 --> 00:45:15.550
And it was remarkable how much
it just helped me recenter.

00:45:18.270 --> 00:45:23.150
So I could show up again and (indistinct)

00:45:23.150 --> 00:45:25.400
to offer the space for others

00:45:25.400 --> 00:45:29.770
to work through and move forward.

00:45:29.770 --> 00:45:30.603
Yeah.

00:45:30.603 --> 00:45:32.790
- Yeah. Such an effective practice.

00:45:32.790 --> 00:45:34.475
And it's also (indistinct)

00:45:34.475 --> 00:45:36.730
that's it's full circle
because it's the self-awareness

00:45:36.730 --> 00:45:39.410
that you're also encouraging others

00:45:39.410 --> 00:45:41.310
to apply in the classroom.

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:45.690
So sometimes when we are
trying to share insights

00:45:45.690 --> 00:45:48.340
with others, it's difficult
to apply it to ourselves.

00:45:49.704 --> 00:45:51.533
And that's also encouraging.

00:45:53.200 --> 00:45:58.200
- I think it's important to
normalize those practices.

00:45:58.300 --> 00:46:03.170
So not too long ago, I
went to visit my doctor.

00:46:03.170 --> 00:46:05.263
Well, I had a doctor's appointment,

00:46:07.035 --> 00:46:10.480
the doctor actually was
coming on her day off,

00:46:10.480 --> 00:46:14.483
because I was in, and she said,

00:46:16.237 --> 00:46:17.590
"Would it be okay with you

00:46:17.590 --> 00:46:22.590
if we take three minutes to
just do a breathing exercise?"

00:46:23.150 --> 00:46:24.770
And I said, "Okay, I don't mind."

00:46:24.770 --> 00:46:28.270
So we sat in the office,

00:46:28.270 --> 00:46:31.577
and we did a breathing
exercise and later she said,

00:46:31.577 --> 00:46:33.214
"This is more for me.

00:46:33.214 --> 00:46:38.214
I'm having an off day
and before I could give,

00:46:38.650 --> 00:46:40.137
I needed to do this."

00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:49.230
I felt healed before she
talked about my blood work.

00:46:49.230 --> 00:46:52.523
I was like, I don't need to
know anything, I feel good.

00:46:54.340 --> 00:46:56.700
- Wow. That's so powerful and encouraging.

00:46:56.700 --> 00:46:59.950
I mean, I've never had
that type of experience

00:46:59.950 --> 00:47:02.787
in a doctor's office, but
it goes such a long way.

00:47:02.787 --> 00:47:06.070
And it makes it full circle
too, because I have had

00:47:06.070 --> 00:47:09.550
students who have shared
that with me before,

00:47:09.550 --> 00:47:11.070
shared that with other, like you said,

00:47:11.070 --> 00:47:12.360
if someone comes sleep deprived

00:47:12.360 --> 00:47:13.230
or there's something going on,

00:47:13.230 --> 00:47:15.530
they're just not their
ultimate selves that day.

00:47:15.530 --> 00:47:19.980
Just for them to hear that
from us goes such a long way.

00:47:19.980 --> 00:47:21.260
And the way you just
characterized that story,

00:47:21.260 --> 00:47:22.640
I think brought it home.

00:47:22.640 --> 00:47:24.730
In a way that's hard to
sometimes conceptualize

00:47:24.730 --> 00:47:27.310
when you're the one sharing those pieces,

00:47:27.310 --> 00:47:30.580
but just to have yeah, that's amazing.

00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:33.590
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah.

00:47:33.590 --> 00:47:34.683
I also--

00:47:34.683 --> 00:47:36.483
- Continue to learn from each other.

00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:40.483
- I do an exercise with students.

00:47:42.640 --> 00:47:46.940
It's called a silent visit with yourself.

00:47:46.940 --> 00:47:51.940
And so I talk with them
about just the nervous system

00:47:52.590 --> 00:47:55.940
and how the nervous system
sometimes goes into autopilot

00:47:55.940 --> 00:48:00.940
and so work, school and
then class, class, class.

00:48:01.130 --> 00:48:04.640
And so what I do really,
it's a one minute exercise

00:48:04.640 --> 00:48:07.410
at the beginning of the class, and I say,

00:48:07.410 --> 00:48:12.410
take a minute to just
pause and remind yourself

00:48:12.520 --> 00:48:16.923
why being here today is important for you.

00:48:18.490 --> 00:48:22.443
And so, students they sit,
sometimes they close their eyes,

00:48:24.129 --> 00:48:25.860
but they're there, they're present,

00:48:25.860 --> 00:48:27.600
the whole thing's about...

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:32.600
And I had to miss class one
time and I told the students

00:48:33.080 --> 00:48:38.080
there's gonna be someone who
will cover for me and so on.

00:48:38.120 --> 00:48:41.040
And the students were not concerned

00:48:41.040 --> 00:48:43.990
that I was gonna miss class,
they were concerned with,

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:48.950
what about the one moment, can
we still do the one moment?

00:48:48.950 --> 00:48:53.950
And I was so touched that that
was so important for them.

00:48:54.130 --> 00:48:56.980
And later on, I asked
them to give feedback,

00:48:56.980 --> 00:48:58.920
and they said that really helped them.

00:48:58.920 --> 00:49:02.610
There's something about
talking with their brain,

00:49:02.610 --> 00:49:06.330
negotiating with the brain
that helped them focus

00:49:06.330 --> 00:49:10.773
on the what's to come. So, yeah.

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:13.570
- Yeah, that's really encouraging to hear,

00:49:13.570 --> 00:49:15.620
and it seems like the practice, it stuck.

00:49:17.884 --> 00:49:19.790
In a way, maybe it was good for them

00:49:19.790 --> 00:49:21.407
to be able to have you gone to say,

00:49:21.407 --> 00:49:25.227
"Okay, well, we can also
incorporate this ourselves,"

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:28.350
even without you around.

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:31.380
Which I guess is a measure
of success for you as well

00:49:31.380 --> 00:49:32.923
to really have it integrated.

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:37.300
Well, this has been so
refreshing in a lot of ways,

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:39.070
maybe in some surprising ways,

00:49:39.070 --> 00:49:40.990
if people were tuning
in and thinking about

00:49:40.990 --> 00:49:42.700
trauma-informed teaching,

00:49:42.700 --> 00:49:44.730
not necessarily expecting to have

00:49:44.730 --> 00:49:47.550
a sense of refreshing aspects,

00:49:47.550 --> 00:49:48.610
and it was some of these practices,

00:49:48.610 --> 00:49:51.080
but I think there's so many
things that are full circle,

00:49:51.080 --> 00:49:52.700
things that we can
learn from our students,

00:49:52.700 --> 00:49:55.500
and that can be vice
versa to really help us

00:49:55.500 --> 00:49:57.920
all be in a better place holistically.

00:49:57.920 --> 00:50:01.270
So again, that really ties
into what we do on the podcast.

00:50:01.270 --> 00:50:03.340
I appreciate you being here to share that,

00:50:03.340 --> 00:50:04.720
but did also want to close.

00:50:04.720 --> 00:50:06.740
And I know you talk about
hope in your work a lot too,

00:50:06.740 --> 00:50:09.200
just having you share what gives you hope

00:50:09.200 --> 00:50:10.230
in the work that you do

00:50:10.230 --> 00:50:12.963
and even as we continue to move forward.

00:50:14.290 --> 00:50:19.290
- Yeah. Thank you, that's
a brilliant question.

00:50:20.610 --> 00:50:25.203
I would say, yeah, people give me hope,

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:29.980
the students, their desire to learn,

00:50:33.360 --> 00:50:38.040
their desire to do right
by their fellow students,

00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:42.297
their honesty and compassion for me,

00:50:45.073 --> 00:50:49.230
I talk about the classroom as a sanctuary

00:50:49.230 --> 00:50:52.810
and it has (indistinct) for me

00:50:52.810 --> 00:50:57.810
and the kindness and grace my students

00:50:59.744 --> 00:51:03.780
have just given me over the years,

00:51:03.780 --> 00:51:05.633
that gives me a lot of hope.

00:51:08.860 --> 00:51:12.660
People are resilient, we are resilient.

00:51:12.660 --> 00:51:16.280
Rumi says, "You are not
a drop in the ocean,

00:51:16.280 --> 00:51:19.530
you're the entire ocean in a drop."

00:51:19.530 --> 00:51:24.530
And we all are that, and we
sometimes lose touch with that.

00:51:25.920 --> 00:51:29.713
And it's the community,

00:51:31.690 --> 00:51:36.504
our fellow human beings that
remind us of wait a second,

00:51:36.504 --> 00:51:41.504
I do matter and you do
matter. And we move forward.

00:51:44.510 --> 00:51:48.203
So there's a lot to, sure,

00:51:51.580 --> 00:51:56.580
there's a lot of pain
and trauma and anxiety,

00:51:57.030 --> 00:52:02.030
and I will never undermine
it and grief and sorrow.

00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:04.810
And at the same time,

00:52:04.810 --> 00:52:09.810
there are pockets of
remarkable beauty and love,

00:52:10.700 --> 00:52:12.643
and those give me hope.

00:52:14.360 --> 00:52:16.070
- So well said.

00:52:16.070 --> 00:52:18.420
In a lot of ways, ties
back to community as well.

00:52:18.420 --> 00:52:20.756
I mean, just to being in a community,

00:52:20.756 --> 00:52:24.480
to be able to engage and
have that sense of hope

00:52:24.480 --> 00:52:27.910
as you see the students'
passions and enthusiasm,

00:52:27.910 --> 00:52:31.000
and as you see their
resilience, so a lot of ways,

00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:32.610
that's really encouraging to hear,

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:36.193
while acknowledging the
realities as well. So.

00:52:37.150 --> 00:52:37.983
- Absolutely.

00:52:39.530 --> 00:52:42.110
- Well, thank you again for
being here on the Addy Hour,

00:52:42.110 --> 00:52:44.020
a very rich conversation,

00:52:44.020 --> 00:52:45.720
which doesn't surprise me in the least,

00:52:45.720 --> 00:52:48.060
and just grateful for the
work that you continue to do.

00:52:48.060 --> 00:52:50.300
And just for the impact
that you're making,

00:52:50.300 --> 00:52:52.110
I know that ripples are continuing

00:52:52.110 --> 00:52:53.490
and they'll continue for quite a while,

00:52:53.490 --> 00:52:56.870
and I'm excited to see how
things continue to move forward

00:52:56.870 --> 00:52:58.573
for you in so many important ways.

00:52:59.761 --> 00:53:00.840
- Thank you so much.

00:53:00.840 --> 00:53:03.080
Thank you for the work
you do for advocating

00:53:03.080 --> 00:53:05.470
for mental health.

00:53:05.470 --> 00:53:10.030
And I'm really grateful to
have spent this past hour

00:53:10.030 --> 00:53:12.980
with you and your audience.

00:53:12.980 --> 00:53:16.471
- Likewise. So encouraging.
Thanks so much.

00:53:16.471 --> 00:53:19.054
(upbeat music)

